act":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State, per fetch arm in the multi-project fan-out, the disposition surfaced when one project's query fails while siblings succeed (isolated/retryable-skip vs whole-pass-fatal), and how the snapshot union treats a project that failed to fetch this pass.","cohort":["E3","F4","G5","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.7969,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'the inbound fetcher builds its JQL pair from one key and fails closed on an empty one (fetcher.jqls_for)'","'Multi-project inbound fetch — fan-out + snapshot union; per-query truncation ceiling scoped per project'","ABSENCE: no disposition word (transient/retryable vs fatal/permanent) is attached to a per-project fetch-arm failure within the fan-out"],"finding":"The plan relaxes the inbound fetcher from a single JQL pair to a per-project fan-out ('Multi-project inbound fetch — fan-out + snapshot union'), but never states the disposition when ONE project's query fails while sibling projects succeed — e.g. a project deleted remotely, an auth error scoped to one project, or a per-project truncation-ceiling hit. Is a single project's fetch failure fatal to the whole pass (aborting the other projects' ingest) or isolated (the pass proceeds with the reachable projects)? The old single-key path 'fails closed on an empty one'; fanning out multiplies the arms without stating which disposition each arm surfaces.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fdc080405e8d3261b","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Chi